        "reasoning": "Implementing a normalized half-open interval set:\n\n```python\nclass RangeSet:\n    def __init__(self, ranges=()):\n        self._ranges = self._normalize(ranges)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _check_int(value):\n        if type(value) is not int:\n            raise TypeError(...)\n\n    def _validate_pair(self, start, end):\n        self._check_int(start); self._check_int(end)\n        if start > end:\n            raise ValueError\n        return start, end\n\n    def _normalize(self, ranges):\n        # collect, validate, sort, merge\n        out = []\n        for start, end in ranges:\n            start, end = self._validate_pair(start, end)\n            if start < end:\n                out.append((start, end))\n        out.sort()\n        merged = []\n        for s, e in out:\n            if merged and s <= merged[-1][1]:\n                merged[-1] = (merged[-1][0], max(merged[-1][1], e))\n            else:\n                merged.append((s, e))\n        return merged\n    ...\n```\n\nConstructor validation: invalid pairs inside the constructor should probably raise an exception (TypeError/ValueError) \u2014 \"invalid add/remove must not change state\" is specified only for add/remove; for the constructor, it's reasonable to raise an exception.\n\nadd: validate, and if empty do nothing, otherwise normalize(self._ranges + [(start,end)]) \u2014 a new list so no aliasing issue; the internal list is replaced.\n\nremove: validate, if empty do nothing; compute the result by splitting each range.\n\ncontains: strictly check int, and perform a binary search or linear scan.\n\nclip: validate the pair; if empty, return []. Compute the intersection and normalize it (since they're already non-overlapping, the intersection of each range with the clip window becomes non-overlapping non-adjacent segments \u2192 already normalized, just filter). However, if start==end, return an empty list.\n\nranges(): return list(self._ranges) \u2014 a new list. The tuples are immutable so no problem.\n\nAlso, if the input pair has an incorrect length, it will raise an exception during unpacking \u2014 that's fine.\n"
